    description: "FOUND 2026-08-06 while landing PR #227 (INV-linub's java half). PRE-EXISTING and\ndeliberately NOT folded into that PR: widening the diff to cover it would have\nmixed a measured single-branch fix with an unmeasured analyzer-wide behaviour\nchange. Filed instead of fixed silently.\n\nTHE EXPOSURE. _register_sanitizer_callers matches a sanitizer by SHORT NAME and\nthen applies three escapes, in order:\n    1. an exact qualified_name match wins;\n    2. call_construct == 'method'  -> skip;\n    3. the name is in ambiguous_names -> skip.\nFor java, escape 2 can NEVER fire, because no java edge carries the key. So the\nonly thing standing between a short-name collision and a registered barrier is\nthe ambiguous_names list.\n\nTHE LIVE SHAPE, not hypothetical. The WI-tihup branch (receiver IS an imported\nclass) emits unresolved_name = f'{receiver_name}.{method_name}', e.g.\n'Cipher.doFinal'. The sanitizer index really does hold 'Cipher.doFinal' as a key\n(verified: keys are ['Cipher.doFinal', 'doFinal',\n'javax.crypto.Cipher.doFinal']). That is NOT an exact qualified_name match --\nthe sanitizer's qualified_name is 'javax.crypto.Cipher.doFinal' -- so escape 1\nmisses, escape 2 is dead, and 'doFinal' is not in java's ambiguous_names. The\nbarrier registers on name agreement alone, with no check that the receiver is\nactually a javax.crypto.Cipher.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS IN THIS DIRECTION. A registered barrier earns the sanitized label, and a\nsanitized flow is DROPPED from a claim's violation set (#214). So this deletes\nfindings rather than adding false ones -- the expensive direction for a security\ntool, and the one _register_sanitizer_callers's own docstring calls out: 'a\nphantom barrier is never registered from a bare-name collision -- which would\nsilently SUPPRESS a real taint flow (a false negative, worse than a missed\nbarrier for a security tool)'. That protection is simply absent for java.\n\nNOT MEASURED, and this decides whether it is a curiosity or a headline: how many\njava edges on a real repo have a callee short name that collides with a\ncatalogued sanitizer. Java ships exactly ONE sanitizer\n(javax.crypto.Cipher.doFinal), so the blast radius is bounded by how often\n'doFinal' and 'Cipher.doFinal' appear as callee names -- plausibly near zero on\nmost repos, but the cohort holds no java repo that was successfully analysed\n(spring-petclinic timed out). SIZE IT BEFORE FIXING.\n\nTHE FIX IS NOT 'set it everywhere'. call_construct='method' is only true where\nthe call genuinely has a receiver; asserting it on a bare or static call would\nbe a different falsehood, and would also suppress legitimate function-kind sink\nmatches through gate_named_entry's kind filter. Each java emit branch needs the\nquestion answered separately. Scope that per branch, do not sweep.\n\nRELATED: INV-linub (class), and PR #227's typed-local branch which already\ncarries the flag correctly."  # bd44
